Examples of contamination and overkill cases in the SED-fitting-based sample purification schemes. The left and right panels show the best-fit SEDs from Le Phare (used in S1) and EAZY-py (used in S2), respectively; the inset panels show the corresponding photometric redshift ( z ph z_{m ph} ) probability distribution functions (PDF). In all plots, the photometric data points and the upper limits that construct the SED are based on photometry described in Appendix B.1 . In this illustration, a reduced- χ 2 \chi^{2} threshold of χ ν 2 = 3 \chi_{
u}^{2}=3 is adopted, and the dropouts below or above this threshold are marked as “Pass” or “Fail”, respectively. The upper two rows show two F090W dropouts, one being an interloper at z spec = 1.33 z_{m spec}=1.33 and the other being a confirmed galaxy at z spec = 7.29 z_{m spec}=7.29 , while the lower two rows show two F115W dropouts, one being an interloper at z spec = 4.39 z_{m spec}=4.39 and the other being a confirmed galaxy at z spec = 8.52 z_{m spec}=8.52 . In both interloper cases, they have good high- z z solutions with χ ν 2 ≤ 3 \chi_{
u}^{2}\leq 3 and narrow PDF in both S1 and S2 and would therefore be retained, i.e., they are cases of contamination. On the other hand, the two confirmed high- z z galaxies have χ ν 2 > 3 \chi_{
u}^{2}>3 and would be rejected by both schemes, i.e., they are cases of overkill.

